# Troubleshooting

## Problem 1 — Brute-Force Rule Fired on Every Failed Login

### Cause

The initial rule used:

```xml
<if_sid>5760</if_sid>
```

without any frequency or timeframe correlation.

As a result, every authentication failure generated the custom alert.

### Solution

Frequency and source correlation were added:

```xml
frequency="6"
timeframe="120"
```

together with:

```xml
<if_matched_sid>5760</if_matched_sid>
<same_source_ip />
```

The detection then represented a repeated authentication pattern rather than a single failed login.

---

## Problem 2 — XSS Detection Required an Existing Web-Attack Match

The XSS rule was intentionally chained to Wazuh's existing web-attack rule:

```xml
<if_sid>31105</if_sid>
```

This means the custom rule depends on the underlying web-attack detection successfully identifying the request.

Therefore, when troubleshooting the custom XSS rule, both levels need to be checked:

```text
Apache access.log
        ↓
Apache decoder
        ↓
Rule 31105
        ↓
Rule 100012
```

If rule `31105` does not match, rule `100012` will not fire.

